  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Boititap Korenyo: The Wealth of Our Land</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This collaboration enables the Ogiek people in Kenya&apos;s Mount Elgon to combine their immense knowledge and skills in managing their customary land with new community-based Geographic Information Science (GIS) technologies. The project employs a revolutionary tech-based solution called MAPEO, which enables them to collect and leverage data describing land and resource use in order to defend andadvance their land rights. The project aims to:

# Build the Ogiek#s capacity to map and monitor their ancestral lands.
# Reduce the Ogiek#s marginalisation through increasing their ability to advocate for themselves in local and national platforms with government and other officials.
# Secure greater land tenure for the Ogiek through the existing legal mechanisms in Kenya, reduce territorialconflict with neighbouring communities, and increase internal fluency on territory issues for indigenous people.

The  project is implemented by a consortium lead by the Chepkitale Indigenous Peoples Development Project (CIPDP), as a representative organisation of the Ogiek people of Mount Elgon. CIPDP has been working on land rights issues for a number of years, and since 2010 with Forest Peoples Programme on issues including capacity development for policy influencing and strategic institutional support; GIS training; responsible finance; and networking with neighbouring communities. Digital Democracy is a long term partner of FPP, having collaborated with them in Latin America providing technical support and local partner training in mapping and land defense technologies in theAmazon.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="90">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CHEPKITALE INDIGENOUS PEOPLE</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Chepkitale Indigenous People Development Project (CIPD) was founded in 2000 and registered in 2003. CIPDP was founded from the needof the community to self-organise and supporting the collective pursuing the land rights of the Ogiek Community. All its programmesare fully community initiated and driven. What is unique about CIPDP is that its founders are community activists who inherited thestruggle of the community from past generations and are committed to inspire a new generation tokeep striving forthe vision set forth by their ancestors.

CIPDPs programmes are centred around protecting the land rights of the Ogiek Community in Mt. Elgon, this include: awareness raising on all aspects of their land rights, policy influencing, providing legal aid to members of the community, promote community-led environmental conservation, and support the community to develop sustainable livelihoods.

CIPDP works in Mt. Elgon region traversing the Kenyan Bungoma and Trans-Nzoia counties where there is presence of the Ogiek Community members. They haveestablished working relationships with communities especially hunter gatherer communities whom we work together on land and resource rights, and alliances with other like-minded national and international civil society organizations.</narrative>
  </participating-org>
